#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "e1c7a206-77db-55e2-8b0e-cde3e9f1b489" :question/math-academy-id "e-16539" :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2},\n\\qquad\na_1 = 2, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1, \n\\quad\nn \\geq 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2,$ and $a_2 = 1,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5{a}_{2}+{a}_{1}=5\\cdot 1+2=5+2=7 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =5{a}_{3}+{a}_{2}=5\\cdot 7+1=35+1=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128820 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "17f9c357-b103-5a51-95ad-ad0ea0b1fba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-16541" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n\n- $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n\n- $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n,$ and $s_i$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}.$\n\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1},$ $c_{n-2},$ and $c_{n-3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128821 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "493a826d-4f6f-55e2-9d6a-a0909d34a483" :question/math-academy-id "e-16555" :question/problem "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1},\n\\qquad\na_1 =0, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1,\n\\quad\nn \\geq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation $a_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are  constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}.$ So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}},$\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4529d2-0223-539a-84d3-7092b35719fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126835} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5feb8b-f9e5-5920-96e0-1fb160219341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126836} {:answer/id #uuid "09f9aa59-f57a-5d9e-a005-f8606e5cc298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126837} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff68374-0b27-55d1-8491-135021da6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126838} {:answer/id #uuid "3718cdf7-b4db-5d90-bb3d-6365fad82a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ff68374-0b27-55d1-8491-135021da6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126838} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b45c6fd-dfb0-5465-a53a-87f5f66006ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b8ee369-c2d6-57ab-995b-02dacf3c692c" :question/math-academy-id "q-252990" :question/problem "Find the fifth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3,\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the first term ${a}_{1}=5,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fifth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-3=2\\cdot 5-3=7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2{a}_{2}-3=2\\cdot 7-3=11 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2{a}_{3}-3=2\\cdot 11-3=19 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2{a}_{4}-3=2\\cdot 19-3=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fifth term is ${a}_{5}=35.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809c5305-7c59-5f1e-a720-17ad45f5e429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126847} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff3683b-ad3f-570c-b561-b8b7a1b4c235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126848} {:answer/id #uuid "1e47823f-a7a6-5c3a-8c0d-5e17b23fce71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126849} {:answer/id #uuid "1e55e843-9cf0-546d-97f6-b08be7b5f728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126850} {:answer/id #uuid "1e61e457-cc77-5e0b-8fe6-4d4022ebdcc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809c5305-7c59-5f1e-a720-17ad45f5e429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126847} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2fe481-7ac0-5b95-b675-2018c8daa317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13864329-1fa0-567c-b5b4-57dd540d6c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-253143" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}^{2}+2{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=2{c}_{n-1}+n$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acfd1a4b-8a3a-578c-9b26-541bc83beeb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300719} {:answer/id #uuid "bf61f159-fabf-5cb6-8bcf-f4d03c42ef03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300720} {:answer/id #uuid "873f2c05-12cf-5210-962f-d1c4ebe188a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300721} {:answer/id #uuid "006c188d-38c8-5e26-a134-a13fb7cefd5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300722} {:answer/id #uuid "04117c07-cab8-5239-966a-b01e9b7ec735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006c188d-38c8-5e26-a134-a13fb7cefd5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300722} :answer-field/id #uuid "040ccf82-071a-5447-bab7-f9a303883c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6af1f1dd-0830-5336-97d9-6d826a433e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-253144" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}+5{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=4{c}_{n-1}+7n$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $7n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379ba3d9-25e8-5fbf-9a5b-4cde566991e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726} {:answer/id #uuid "10678c62-50b9-5bbe-8b30-666f12e6dce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300727} {:answer/id #uuid "314e7856-0e6c-50eb-8adb-9b043a593ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300728} {:answer/id #uuid "96ecc926-5973-51f2-99a6-0a18109b4906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300729} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbe80c-2393-5287-b57b-3fe224dc79de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "379ba3d9-25e8-5fbf-9a5b-4cde566991e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47559a0-c620-598f-9655-65d42f6ac64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "811efd1e-d1e9-59d7-973e-5934a7f5d06a" :question/math-academy-id "q-253349" :question/problem "Find the third term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}-1,\\,{a}_{1}=-4,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the first term ${a}_{1}=-4,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the third term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =-4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3{a}_{1}-1=3\\cdot (-4)-1=-13 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3{a}_{2}-1=3\\cdot (-13)-1=-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term is ${a}_{3}=-40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d321f5da-b2c7-5ed5-861e-c578a02c33b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300733} {:answer/id #uuid "348509c2-a947-5d12-9a2b-c0095156c112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300734} {:answer/id #uuid "412436bd-1d31-549d-8d9a-878b039821b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300735} {:answer/id #uuid "96e16b1f-5d30-5b54-be75-811e5c304bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300736} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b8f290-1b9a-5333-bc7b-8a8346a292c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d321f5da-b2c7-5ed5-861e-c578a02c33b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0519efba-7220-5394-8681-7c287a7b9132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fe8dc59-939f-58df-a247-260dc322c4d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-253350" :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-5,\\,{a}_{1}=6,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the first term ${a}_{1}=6,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =6 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-5=2\\cdot 6-5=7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2{a}_{2}-5=2\\cdot 7-5=9 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2{a}_{3}-5=2\\cdot 9-5=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is ${a}_{4}=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf3c0e87-86c9-569c-9b54-21791dffac8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739} {:answer/id #uuid "b652adf0-54f7-5293-9e14-035c3b0372ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300740} {:answer/id #uuid "94660bfb-2590-5cc4-8400-8bad66214db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300741} {:answer/id #uuid "065ea280-509d-586a-ad25-edeed69796e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300742} {:answer/id #uuid "28b99bc5-85b9-5adc-8000-e95b0b1253ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065ea280-509d-586a-ad25-edeed69796e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300742} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd551d0f-318d-5d7a-9755-6809cbc818aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3af01d4c-e663-5fb4-9f38-8aac09cc1496" :question/math-academy-id "q-253351" :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms ${a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{2}=4,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3{a}_{2}+2{a}_{1}=3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2=12+4=16 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =3{a}_{3}+2{a}_{2}=3\\cdot 16+2\\cdot 4=48+8=56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is ${a}_{4}=56.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1cddf0f-7cc0-558d-9900-2029ea3ce65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126869} {:answer/id #uuid "6db6f480-6cbd-5e80-a67d-08b774ab8127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db6f480-6cbd-5e80-a67d-08b774ab8127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126870} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cb1333-27b5-5512-96b7-08ea4118ada4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed515486-7861-5ce5-99ac-f4ebdf3f944a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126872} {:answer/id #uuid "04a5c128-1449-5f6c-bcc8-5be4adfb3973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126873} {:answer/id #uuid "215aea16-b6d7-5359-94d3-b246eb3017a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215aea16-b6d7-5359-94d3-b246eb3017a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126874} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed846eb-0582-5acd-afa4-700047cc14ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2a0798-98bf-5cc2-a77c-3201c411f3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126876} {:answer/id #uuid "772d1532-2c47-5613-b4f6-2f12c29f547f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "772d1532-2c47-5613-b4f6-2f12c29f547f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126877} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaad6d1-20f3-5853-b984-52c9b470ef80" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "958c8b51-02a6-562a-b91d-0cb22577bebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-253360" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f6bead-4306-5ced-b37f-0a8a83f20b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300746} {:answer/id #uuid "bcaceabd-e8d3-596b-9a56-33eefe1d4661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300747} {:answer/id #uuid "52d1aa58-97fb-55ad-a869-07988b49bac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcaceabd-e8d3-596b-9a56-33eefe1d4661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300747} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9600b00-1da4-5f71-8362-133281a7fdbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b904adc-8544-507b-8bae-b05f08785a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300750} {:answer/id #uuid "ded33bcd-58e6-5906-b180-1de8d88ac318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b904adc-8544-507b-8bae-b05f08785a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300750} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb70289d-66d3-592d-8dfb-fc38a3de0fec" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ca8b64-d7ab-5786-b46d-358bba6a9150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300753} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa52140-e347-5780-80f6-be87c67e37fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfa52140-e347-5780-80f6-be87c67e37fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c65aa76-4fd2-594e-bc83-d859473c1027" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1ec1408-7504-5b11-90fb-9d3bb0345917" :question/math-academy-id "q-253362" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520ed414-3d79-5b86-852e-9d758cdc56a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300757} {:answer/id #uuid "70360374-78bb-5947-9e1f-21eea9c50508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300758} {:answer/id #uuid "c4823a22-7188-549a-99e1-77770cc23d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70360374-78bb-5947-9e1f-21eea9c50508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300758} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8afc7a-182e-502a-95c4-528b1b58aca2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7901bf-81f7-5f5e-bc19-0471ee881928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300761} {:answer/id #uuid "9abba510-3c15-5fca-8fa1-51db45651ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af7901bf-81f7-5f5e-bc19-0471ee881928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300761} :answer-field/id #uuid "310624bd-0216-5c21-a92c-8de20c683bbc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4b013c8-0ffa-5829-b57f-0d288c62929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300764} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5354f1-b228-5ea0-a3d7-2546d856f7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5354f1-b228-5ea0-a3d7-2546d856f7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0316caa-8c2d-570c-b1dc-3b21e0316898" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "347005bc-29fd-55a3-8e95-980ad7df9f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253365" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n.$ \n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$  and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=n$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae2939b-0bfa-557c-947d-5d35d42a4325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300768} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5e5e9a-1d6a-5b14-996a-aadde4fd4388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300769} {:answer/id #uuid "515b3af5-9d32-5639-9ba4-08e791415799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300770} {:answer/id #uuid "e965260f-a741-5aa2-93c0-30beebd1589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300771} {:answer/id #uuid "41e51de5-32ef-5689-8039-48466677d438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5e5e9a-1d6a-5b14-996a-aadde4fd4388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300769} :answer-field/id #uuid "25acd4fb-fb46-5c5a-b1b3-9368c3803b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87e600a5-bf20-5b52-8f26-9ba11cc84dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-253458" :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n-1}+\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=6,\\,{a}_{2}=3,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms ${a}_{1}=6,$ and ${a}_{2}=3,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =6 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3=\\frac{7}{4}+1=\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is ${a}_{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23280fef-78ec-549d-bcaa-c6e571df17e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300775} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb47e66-f818-55d0-8acd-af323ae32217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300776} {:answer/id #uuid "42c55047-f997-5ea6-bd36-4da4ac949992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300777} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd9e09c-3af1-5357-a194-5ad2d142ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300778} {:answer/id #uuid "e15733f7-a966-55ef-bb08-6687b8f6f821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd9e09c-3af1-5357-a194-5ad2d142ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300778} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c7aabb-6f2a-5ed2-8170-e3cf7fa1d02a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa8f043-0108-5d57-9b6a-3ff01feefed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253467" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{2}+3$\n- ${b}_{n}=2{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}+\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38f5400-d221-566e-ba0f-6a50498ca8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300782} {:answer/id #uuid "afe60291-e78e-5362-ba78-9b4c9aea13c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300783} {:answer/id #uuid "7afe82ec-f00b-5e08-92f9-aea8ca69bc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300784} {:answer/id #uuid "6c37f06d-565a-5a5c-a024-fad7ef60f03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300785} {:answer/id #uuid "e371e702-80df-5928-a49d-8be0fad5bd09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7afe82ec-f00b-5e08-92f9-aea8ca69bc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300784} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf99fd1-7c68-5365-8973-de32cc1695e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21a536a7-eaf5-542f-8447-68faf609e75b" :question/math-academy-id "q-253468" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{c}_{n-1}}{3}-2{c}_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1269aed-048a-5327-ba68-e236ed687459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300789} {:answer/id #uuid "b00fbc66-decb-5218-94b4-c50b9abb3a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300790} {:answer/id #uuid "b690c061-d802-51db-b28e-bd26f6757e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300791} {:answer/id #uuid "9084c9f5-8844-5530-96c0-ead88cd2759f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300792} {:answer/id #uuid "15d6f5e5-a822-5ab0-b237-a155e9613033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1269aed-048a-5327-ba68-e236ed687459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d519759-a179-55ef-8eb8-921a715ea0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40a9896d-2592-5ccb-afef-6c9ca025eef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-253485" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=4{b}_{n-1}+{n}^{2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}+6{c}_{n-2}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term ${n}^{2}$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f175f109-8d63-5205-9ff1-33c56944939a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300796} {:answer/id #uuid "21c4eea8-ca79-528e-b7c3-ba2c46c56eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300797} {:answer/id #uuid "14fbc438-3fbe-579b-8fda-0379c186fe64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c4eea8-ca79-528e-b7c3-ba2c46c56eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300797} :answer-field/id #uuid "298b4510-4f77-5e35-8e5d-931d0f61fc3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124dac3c-7ae2-5c7d-8a0e-53ce59bb196d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300800} {:answer/id #uuid "9a292418-dfde-529d-95ff-5ba0f561b35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a292418-dfde-529d-95ff-5ba0f561b35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300801} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fa8cd6-07f0-57d2-a489-b54e4fa286d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d2fc3a8-ae2c-5fb1-941a-53137f621434" :question/math-academy-id "q-265142" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and \nis\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-2}$ appears in the second power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is\n$\\text{non-linear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e53ce2e6-a60e-5c5c-a3b4-4b0405b1724b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300804} {:answer/id #uuid "375a1658-bbb2-5636-bf00-60025931bb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300805} {:answer/id #uuid "85239fda-c735-5857-b463-77ba076561d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375a1658-bbb2-5636-bf00-60025931bb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300805} :answer-field/id #uuid "700d12af-3d2b-5464-9e39-43e633fb44fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c8bb5a-6f36-5d5a-988d-664b2ab4be34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300808} {:answer/id #uuid "460a8993-abca-5ba6-a4d3-f6133ee6ed2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "460a8993-abca-5ba6-a4d3-f6133ee6ed2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300809} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a0bbf2-ca24-5cf0-922b-2ef5a634c9ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d88310da-b7f6-564c-a97c-433385879744" :question/math-academy-id "q-265152" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and\nis\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The term ${a}_{n-1}$ is not in the first power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is $\\text{non-linear}.$"}]}
